# /usr/data/anvil/anvil.conf -- runtime switches. Edit on the printer, reboot.
# PRESERVED across mod updates.

# FIVE SWITCHES USED TO BE IN THIS FILE: MOD_WEB, MOD_CAM, MOD_UI, MOD_WIFI
# and MOD_SSH. All defaulted to 1, and each really offered a partly-installed
# mod. The web stack, the screen and wifi now start because they are
# installed; not starting them is `opkg remove`, or a stock package.
#
# The camera is worth spelling out: mjpg-streamer on :8080, proxied by nginx
# at /webcam/. It is an s6-rc longrun that reports ready only once it has a
# device, so a printer without one waits out its timeout-up on every boot.
# firmwareExe still reads MOD_CAM when it expands the ok-all bundle,
# defaulting to 1 -- add MOD_CAM=0 by hand on a machine that has no camera.
#
# These tune it when there IS one; defaults shown:
#MOD_CAM_RES=1280x720
#MOD_CAM_FPS=30
#MOD_CAM_PORT=8080

# Boot splash: a line of text on the panel from the moment the mod gets
# control until HelixScreen paints. Without it the screen is black for the
# whole of boot. FlashForge's own /usr/prog/start.img cannot fill that gap --
# the lines that would display it are commented out. 0 = leave it black.
MOD_SPLASH=1

# Startup: bin/ff-startup.py, run by the firmwareExe wrapper just before the
# UI. EVERY boot it hands the toolhead boards over from their bootloaders,
# starts klipper, and waits for klipper + moonraker to be ready -- naming on
# the panel whatever is holding things up, and handing the boards over again
# on each retry. MOD_KLIPPER_TRIES bounds the retries.
#
# 0 = do not wait: klipper still starts, the MCU bring-up still happens (it is
# klipper's dependency), and the screen comes up at once in front of a machine
# that may still be connecting. What is skipped is the wait, the retries, the
# panel narration and the first-boot import -- which is also what happens if
# the script or the interpreter is missing, since klipper starting must never
# depend on python.
#MOD_STARTUP_TIMEOUT=240
MOD_STARTUP=1

# First-boot migration, by the same program once the printer is up: it runs
# FF_IMPORT_FIRMWARE_CONFIG and SAVE_CONFIG to move this unit's factory
# calibration into printer.cfg, then stamps .firmware-config-imported. 0 =
# never migrate; the startup wait above still happens.
MOD_IMPORT=1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Priorities. Nice values (0..19) for the services sharing this SoC's two
# cores with klippy. Higher = yields more. 0 disables.
#
# Stock ran neither moonraker nor nginx nor the streamer, so klippy had the
# machine to itself; the mod runs all of them. When the host cannot meet
# Klipper's step-generation deadlines the printer does not slow down, it
# STOPS: the MCU reports "Timer too close" and shuts down mid-job.
#
# These are insurance, not a cure. klippy is already ahead on both counts:
# klipper_pri.sh puts its main thread on SCHED_FIFO 50, which outranks every
# nice value there is, and its serial reader thread calls os.nice(-20) itself.
# So these matter only for whatever klippy thread is neither, and for the case
# where the chrt or the nice did not take. Check that first:
#     grep -i nice /usr/data/logs/printer.log
# "current nice = -20" means it applied; "nice process failed" means it did not.
#
# Neither touches memory or eMMC contention -- this busybox has no ionice.
#
# Moonraker: modest. It is the print path, so starving it delays the UI.
NICE_MOONRAKER=5
# The streamer: the most deserving. Pure background work that nothing waits
# on, and if the camera hands over YUYV rather than MJPEG it encodes every
# frame in software -- the largest CPU load the mod can add. If a print dies
# with "Timer too close" and this is the difference, stop the camera rather
# than reaching for a nicer number.
NICE_CAM=10
# klippy ITSELF, the odd one out: the two above yield TO klippy, this one is
# klippy. 0 is what FlashForge shipped and what you want; it lives here
# because a nice value belongs with the other nice values rather than inside
# the klipperDaemon shim, where it made that file machine-specific.
#
# A negative value is mostly redundant -- SCHED_FIFO 50 already outranks every
# nice value. Set it only if the log says the chrt did not take.
NICE_KLIPPER=0
